paragraph text.

# Sparse Body Autofit

One short paragraph should become comfortably large without looking like a marketing splash page.

# Dense Body Autofit

This slide intentionally contains enough body content to force a smaller body font. The renderer should preserve
readable spacing, keep headings predictable, and avoid clipping content in a typical fullscreen browser viewport.

Autofit should treat this as normal slide content rather than a failure mode. If a slide is too dense even at the
minimum font size, the fallback should be visually obvious and still allow the presenter to reach all content.

The goal is to test the everyday case where a technical presenter adds a little too much detail but still expects the
deck to adapt gracefully on desktop and laptop screens.

# Inline Markdown

Plain text can include emphasis, strong text, combined emphasis, inline code, links (https://example.com/deck), and
autolinks like https://example.com/autolink.

# Lists

- Unordered item with a short label.
- Unordered item with inline emphasis, strong text, and inline code.
- Unordered item with nested structure:
  - Nested unordered item.
  - Nested item with a link (https://example.com/nested).

1. Ordered item one.
2. Ordered item two with a longer sentence that should wrap cleanly.
3. Ordered item three.

# Task Lists

- [ ] Unchecked task item.
- [x] Checked task item.
- [ ] Task item with enough text to wrap across more than one visual line when the browser viewport is narrow.

Task list rendering is a future Markdown extension in some contexts, so this slide also documents the desired visual
target if task markers are supported.

# Block Quotes

> A short quote should read as a distinct block.

> A longer quote should wrap cleanly and preserve the quote treatment across lines. It should not collide with the
> slide number indicator.
>
> - Quoted list item.
